 S~S TruSoft Computing LLC (TSC LLC) Repository Philosophy

------------------------------------------

 S~S  The Philosophy is stated somewhere else.

   The SimpleSal repository README explains how a SimpleSal App, such as this one, is constructed.

   There is additional information available on the "substack.com" site for user "SimpleSal".

------------------------------------------

=====================

  S~S This Repository is not the same as other Repositories

    Different from common industry standards using a repository to control working software.

    In support of the educational content and goals of the SimpleSal ecosystem, this repository is
    used to describe changes over Time made to App software while getting a SimpleSal App to work.

    The idea needs a starting point: the App software using ssTEA fails to build or work.  The
    problem is showing a human the difference between "fails" and "co-exists" over multiple steps.
    The repository management UI, the Repo App, provides this comparison as a basic feature.  Cool.

    Each set of source files contained in a repository "commit" exists somewhere along a path from
    "App fails to build or work" to "App co-exists with ssTEA".  The metadata in RELEASE for each
    commit identifies the point along the path at which the set of source files exists.  Different.

------------------------------------------

=====================

  S~S The Elevator Pitch

------------------------------------------

    The processor in an embedded gadget powers up and runs software that exists in Time and Space.

    When you create a space where virtual objects exist and act over Time, you need a Time Keeper.

    When the software you write and put into a gadget exists over Time, events occur over Time.

    A SimpleSal App co-exists with ssTEA, software that is a Time Keeper and an Event Manager.

    This basic App demonstrates the App and ssTEA co-existing while Events occur over Time.

------------------------------------------

=====================

 S~S This Repository

------------------------------------------

 S~S  What is the purpose of the objects in this repository or repo?

    * The repo contains an example of the simplest or most basic SimpleSal App: make Events occur.
      The difference with the app-time basic SimpleSal App is that ssTEA Time and Data Events are used.
      This App also demonstrates the shared data space between the co-existing App and ssTEA software.

      The App uses the Arduino Built-In LED and the serial port to create a visual display of periodic
      moments in Time occuring.  Variations in the baud rate affect the visual of the serial port.

        * when the App loop function executes, a single Ascii value is displayed each second.
        * when the App loop function executes, the Built-In LED is turned ON every 5 seconds.
        * when the Time Event occurs as configured by the App, the Event turns off the LED
          and causes the Data Event to resume and display a sequence of Ascii characters.
        * when the Data Event occurs as configured by the App and resumed by the Time Event,
           the characters "sent" by the Time Event are displayed (generating a longer Ascii pulse).

      A 1-second periodic serial port LED short pulse; a 5-second periodic serial port longer pulse;
      the Built-in LED is ON for seconds 1, 2, and 3; the built-in LED is OFF for seconds 4 and 5.

      Changing these numbers in the App to 100, 500, and 300 milliseconds changes the blink rate.

    * There are build-time configurations with this App, and with ssTEA.  These variations are
      concerned with the debug and App and ssTEA values displayed to the user via the serial port.

      ssTEA has run-time configurations controlling values displayed while the API and Events occur.

      The RELEASE metadata does not describe the build/run-time changes.  The Repo UI displays them.

    * Quick explanation of any Time value being displayed by the App:

        [flags|days.hours.minutes.seconds.milliseconds.microseconds.nanoseconds.plancks]

        plancks and nanoseconds are not detectable, microseconds accuracy is platform-specific.

------------------------------------------

    * The purpose of indicating moments in Time is to allow an external comparison between what
      the processor (running at a frequency) is calculating, and what is trusted to be correct.

      This is what complex multi-processor chip software does to detect one processor overheating;
      when the processor heats up, it slows down, and its calculation of Time begins to slow also.
      Problem: the processor cannot look at itself and judge whether it is slowing down or not.

      Another processor, known to never overload, provides a "micros ()" value whose rate is constant.
      That processor, visible to all processor, is used to balance both power and frequencies.

      And this is just the first SimpleSal App, on the road to writing signal algorithm software!

------------------------------------------

 S~S  What are the contents of this repository?

   * The managed object is everything, and only that, in the rr\app-events\ directory

       * there is no rr\app-events\SimpleSal\

       * rr\app-events\app-events.ino

       * rr\app-events\mesa*.h   and   ss_configuration.h

         the App depends on SimpleSal; the "mesa*.h" and "ss_configuration.h" file are derived from
         the version of SimpleSal named in the release note (see SimpleSal/simplesal README).

       * rr\app-events\api_ssTEA_*.h

         This is App software that implements interactions with the ssTEA API: perform management.

------------------------------------------

 S~S  What happens in my workspace as a result of interacting with this repository?

    The managed object will be placed into your workspace, along with the human metadata files
    (depending on your ability to configure the repo App to only consider files matching filters).

    The only reason to have a metadata copy locally is if files are being modified for a release.
    Readers still awake will realize the SimpleSal metadata about the directory content will
    match the name and therefore overwrite a conventional software package's local metadata files.
    This is a disadvantage resulting from TSC LLC separating the managed object into a subdirectory.

------------------------------------------

 S~S  What about the version of the SimpleSal software the App co-exists with?

    SimpleSal promises backwards compatibility with older versions; basic features do not disappear.

        This means users should be able to update to new versions, as needed, without side-effects.

    Visit the username SimpleSal's repo named /simplesal, clone the latest "released" version tag.

